To Whom It May Concern:

Pursuant to the Georgia Open Records Act, I hereby request the following records:

GEORGIA OPEN RECORDS ACT REQUEST

Subject: Administrative Processing, Electronic Case-Management Records, Filing/Routing History, Communications, Service/Recipient History, and Related Records — DeKalb County Superior Court Case Nos. 25CV7033, 25PA1140, 26CV3064, and 26PA1024

To the Custodian of Records / Clerk of the Superior Court of DeKalb County:
Pursuant to the Georgia Open Records Act, O.C.G.A. § 50-18-70 et seq., I request access to existing public records concerning the administrative, ministerial, filing, docketing, routing, electronic case-management, service/notification, communications, and records-management handling of the following four identified proceedings in the Superior Court of DeKalb County:
Case No. 25CV7033
Case No. 25PA1140
Case No. 26CV3064
Case No. 26PA1024

This is a new and independent records request. It is not intended to amend, supplement, supersede, or incorporate by reference my prior December 2025 records request concerning the Clerk's Office.

The four identified case numbers are being requested together because they arose during a related period and because the two Poverty Affidavit cases concern applications associated with the two principal proceedings:
25PA1140 — Poverty Affidavit case associated with and filed in connection with 25CV7033, including a copy of the complaint for 25CV7033;

25CV7033 — underlying civil action;

26PA1024 — Poverty Affidavit case associated with and filed in connection with 26CV3064, including a copy of the writ of mandamus for 26CV3064;

26CV3064 — mandamus proceeding.

The requester is not asking the Clerk's Office to determine that these proceedings are legally related cases. The request simply identifies them together and seeks the existing administrative records concerning each proceeding and any existing records documenting administrative connections, cross-references, or interactions among them.


I. PURPOSE AND SCOPE
This request is principally directed toward the administrative and ministerial record surrounding the processing of the identified proceedings, rather than the substantive contents of the pleadings already contained in the case files.

The requester already possesses copies of many of the filings in these proceedings. Accordingly, this request is not principally seeking duplicate copies of pleadings, complaints, motions, affidavits, briefs, or other documents merely because they appear in the public docket.

Instead, the request seeks the underlying administrative records and electronic information that may document:
when a filing was received;
how it was received;
how it entered the Clerk's administrative system;
when and how it was accepted, rejected, returned, held, corrected, indexed, or docketed;
what administrative status was assigned;
how it was routed;
what system user, office, division, or personnel account performed a documented administrative action;
what administrative notes were created;
what communications occurred concerning processing;
what electronic service or notification transactions occurred;
who was added to or removed from service or notification lists;
what case-management or workflow information was maintained;
what changes occurred in the electronic record;
and what policies, procedures, workflows, or system rules governed those administrative actions.

The request does not seek judicial deliberations, judicial mental impressions, draft judicial decisions, legal analysis, or other material protected from disclosure by law.

The request is intended to obtain existing documentary, electronic, database, audit, workflow, communication, and administrative records, and does not ask the Clerk's Office to create a new narrative, chronology, legal opinion, conclusion, or compilation that does not already exist.

Where an existing database, electronic case-management system, filing system, document-management system, service system, or other computer system contains responsive data fields, audit information, transaction histories, timestamps, status information, or other responsive electronic records, those existing records are requested.


II. IDENTIFIED CASES AND ADMINISTRATIVE RELATIONSHIPS
Please process the request separately for each of the four identified case numbers.

For each case, please provide responsive administrative records concerning the life cycle of the case and its filings.

Please also provide any existing records that identify or document administrative relationships among:
25CV7033 and 25PA1140;
26CV3064 and 26PA1024;
25CV7033 and 26CV3064;
25PA1140 and 26PA1024; and
any other combination of the four identified proceedings.

This includes existing:
cross-references;
related-case designations;
administrative case links;
internal case-management references;
docket notes;
routing references;
filing references;
administrative associations;
case-transfer or reassignment records;
system-generated relationships; and
communications referring to one identified case in connection with another.

The request does not ask the Clerk's Office to create a determination that the cases are legally related.


III. CASE CREATION AND ADMINISTRATIVE OPENING
For each of the four identified cases, please provide existing records sufficient to establish, where maintained:
date and time the case was created;
date and time the initiating filing was received;
method of receipt;
filing transaction information;
case-number assignment;
case type or classification;
division or administrative unit assignment;
judge assignment or reassignment, to the extent maintained administratively;
date and time the case was entered into the electronic case-management system;
initial administrative status;
subsequent administrative status changes;
administrative holds;
corrections;
transfers;
reassignments;
closures or reopening events;
and other existing system-generated case events.


IV. FILING RECEIPT, ACCEPTANCE, PROCESSING, AND DOCKETING HISTORY
For filings associated with the identified proceedings, please provide existing administrative records sufficient to establish:
date and time received;
method of receipt;
filing transaction date/time;
receiving office, division, or unit;
acceptance status;
rejection status;
return status;
correction status;
hold status;
date/time of docket entry;
document indexing information;
administrative status;
routing;
subsequent administrative actions;
and any existing reason or status code associated with an administrative action.

Where a filing was rejected, returned, held, corrected, or otherwise subjected to an administrative processing event, please provide existing records documenting that event, including any existing reason code, administrative note, communication, or system-generated status. The request is not asking the Clerk's Office to explain why an event occurred unless that explanation exists in an existing record.


V. ELECTRONIC CASE-MANAGEMENT AND AUDIT HISTORY
For each identified case, please provide existing electronic records or data sufficient to establish, where maintained:
case creation events;
docket activity;
document upload events;
document indexing events;
filing acceptance;
filing rejection;
filing correction;
document replacement;
document version history;
status changes;
assignment;
reassignment;
routing;
administrative holds;
workflow events;
submission or presentation status where maintained administratively;
hearing or scheduling status;
user/action history;
audit history;
transaction history;
workflow history;
system-generated event history;
and other administrative case-management events.

If the system maintains an audit trail, activity history, workflow history, document history, or comparable administrative history that is reasonably retrievable using existing computer programs ordinarily used by the Clerk's Office, please provide that existing information. This request includes existing data fields and system-generated records and is not limited to information displayed on the publicly accessible docket.

VI. FILING LIFECYCLE AND ADMINISTRATIVE CHRONOLOGY
For each significant filing or administrative event in the four cases, please provide existing records sufficient to establish the documented administrative sequence, including, where maintained:
receipt → acceptance/rejection → indexing → docketing → routing → administrative status → subsequent administrative action.

The requester is specifically interested in records that may exist within the Clerk's administrative systems even where substantially similar information appears in the public docket. The existence of information on the public docket should not be treated as a basis for withholding or declining to produce a separate responsive administrative record if the Clerk's systems maintain additional responsive information concerning the processing of the filing.


VII. ADMINISTRATIVE NOTES AND INTERNAL PROCESSING RECORDS
Please provide existing administrative notes, clerk notes, processing notes, workflow notes, routing notes, case-management notes, contact notes, or comparable records concerning the four identified cases, including notes concerning:
filing receipt;
filing deficiencies;
filing acceptance or rejection;
docketing;
routing;
processing;
service;
notification;
case assignment;
reassignment;
administrative holds;
scheduling;
hearing processing;
communications with parties or attorneys concerning administrative matters;
communications with court personnel concerning administrative matters;
and other ministerial handling of the cases.

If an administrative note contains exempt or confidential information, please provide any legally segregable nonexempt portion.


VIII. PERSONNEL, SYSTEM USERS, AND DOCUMENTED ADMINISTRATIVE ACTIONS
Please provide existing records sufficient to identify the Clerk's Office personnel, divisions, offices, or system users associated with documented administrative actions concerning the four identified cases.

Where maintained, please provide:
name;
official title;
office or division;
functional administrative role;
system user identifier or account identifier, where such information is itself a public record and disclosure is lawful;
date or period of documented involvement;
and the administrative action reflected in the record.

The requester does not seek private personnel files or unrelated personal information.
The request is limited to existing records identifying governmental personnel by their official role, office, title, or documented administrative activity.


IX. ELECTRONIC SERVICE, NOTIFICATION LISTS, AND RECIPIENT HISTORY
For 25CV7033 and 26CV3064, please provide existing administrative and electronic records concerning persons, attorneys, law firms, associates, court personnel, judicial staff, Clerk's Office personnel, and other recipients associated with electronic service, notification, filing distribution, or similar electronic communications concerning filings in those cases.

The requester is not asking the Clerk's Office to determine whether any particular person was legally entitled to receive service. The request instead seeks the existing administrative record showing how persons or electronic addresses came to be included in, excluded from, added to, removed from, or otherwise associated with electronic service or notification transactions.

Please provide, where maintained:
persons, offices, attorneys, law firms, associates, court personnel, judicial staff, or electronic addresses associated with service or notification transactions;
date and time each recipient was added or removed;
filing, document, transaction, or administrative event associated with the change;
recipient category or classification;
whether the recipient was associated with a party, attorney, law firm, Clerk's Office, judicial office, court administration, or another category;
source of recipient information;
whether the recipient was added manually, automatically, or through another system-generated process;
Clerk's Office employee, system user, office, or division associated with the documented change, where maintained;
administrative notes concerning the addition, removal, modification, substitution, or correction;
communications concerning such changes;
system-generated audit history or transaction history;
historical versions of service or notification lists, where maintained;
successful, unsuccessful, rejected, returned, or exceptional service/notification status;
transaction identifiers;
changes to recipients following filing, disposition, withdrawal, reassignment, amendment, or other administrative events involving 26CV3064;
changes to recipients in 25CV7033 occurring during or after the pendency or disposition of 26CV3064; and
existing records cross-referencing service or notification recipients between 25CV7033 and 26CV3064.

The request specifically includes existing records concerning persons who were not named parties in the action, including attorneys, associates, law-firm personnel, Clerk's Office personnel, judicial assistants, case-management personnel, courtroom administrators, or other court employees, to the extent such persons appear in service or notification records.

Where a court employee or governmental employee appears as a recipient or service contact, the requester seeks only existing information concerning the person's official role, office, title, or functional position and the administrative event associated with the person's inclusion or removal. No private personnel information is requested.


X. SOURCE AND AUTHORITY FOR CHANGES TO SERVICE OR NOTIFICATION INFORMATION
Please provide existing records sufficient to establish the source of changes to service or notification information, including whether a change was:
generated automatically by the electronic filing system;
entered by a Clerk's Office employee;
initiated by an attorney or party;
initiated by court personnel;
generated by case assignment or reassignment;
generated by attorney appearance or withdrawal;
generated by a change in case status;
generated by another administrative event;
or made through another documented process.

Please provide existing policies, procedures, manuals, training materials, administrative instructions, system documentation, or other guidance concerning:
creation of service or notification lists;
addition of recipients;
removal of recipients;
modification or correction of recipients;
attorney and law-firm service contacts;
court personnel appearing as service or notification recipients;
automatic recipient associations;
manual recipient modifications;
changes resulting from case assignment or reassignment;
changes resulting from attorney appearance or withdrawal;
changes resulting from disposition or withdrawal of motions or related proceedings;
and identification of persons or offices authorized to make such changes.


XI. SERVICE TRANSACTION RECORDS
For filings in 25CV7033 and 26CV3064, please provide existing records sufficient to establish, where maintained:
date/time of service transaction;
filing/document served;
recipients selected;
recipient category or role;
method of electronic service or notification;
successful or failed delivery status;
system-generated transaction identifier;
user/account initiating the transaction;
additions or removals associated with the transaction;
and administrative notes or error/exception records associated with the transaction.


XII. EMAILS AND ELECTRONIC COMMUNICATIONS
Please provide existing emails, electronic messages, correspondence, memoranda, administrative communications, help-desk requests, service requests, routing communications, or other electronic communications concerning the administrative handling of the four identified cases.

The requested communications include communications concerning:
filing;
receipt;
acceptance;
rejection;
return;
correction;
docketing;
routing;
submission;
service;
notification;
recipient lists;
recipient additions or removals;
attorney or associate additions or removals;
court personnel appearing as recipients;
IFP/Poverty Affidavit processing;
case assignment;
reassignment;
administrative holds;
scheduling;
case status;
communications with the plaintiff;
communications with attorneys;
communications between Clerk's Office personnel;
communications with court administration;
and communications referring to one of the four identified cases in connection with another.

For purposes of locating electronic messages, the requested case identifiers are:
25CV7033
25PA1140
26CV3064
26PA1024

Please also search, where reasonably necessary, using the relevant party names, filing identifiers, document descriptions, and the names/titles/offices of personnel responsible for the administrative functions described in this request.

The requester recognizes that a communication may concern a case without including the case number in its subject line or body. The request is limited to communications concerning administrative, ministerial, filing, routing, service, notification, records-management, or other non-deliberative functions. It does not seek judicial deliberations, judicial mental impressions, draft decisions, legal analysis, or protected judicial work product.


XIII. TELEPHONE, CALL, AND CONTACT RECORDS
Please provide existing records maintained in the ordinary course concerning telephone or other direct communications relating to the administrative handling of the four identified cases, including, where maintained:
telephone call logs;
contact logs;
call notes;
message records;
administrative contact notes;
telephone-message slips;
call-routing records;
customer-service or help-desk records;
case-management contact entries;
and other existing records documenting communications concerning filing, processing, service, routing, IFP/Poverty Affidavit processing, or case status.

The requester is not asking the Clerk's Office to create a new call history or reconstruct calls that were not recorded in an existing record.


XIV. POVERTY AFFIDAVIT CASES — 25PA1140 AND 26PA1024
The DeKalb County Superior Court identifies these proceedings as Poverty Affidavit cases, and the case identifiers 25PA1140 and 26PA1024 correspond to those proceedings.

For 25PA1140, which was filed in connection with 25CV7033, and 26PA1024, which was filed in connection with 26CV3064, please provide existing administrative records sufficient to establish:
receipt of the Poverty Affidavit/application;
date and time received;
method of receipt;
case-number assignment;
case creation;
document indexing;
docketing;
administrative classification;
routing;
assignment;
administrative status;
review or processing status, to the extent reflected in administrative records;
acceptance, rejection, return, correction, or hold status;
fee or filing-status processing;
communications concerning administrative deficiencies;
communications concerning processing;
routing to a judge or judicial office, where reflected in existing administrative records;
subsequent administrative status;
and disposition-related administrative records.

Please also provide existing records sufficient to establish the administrative relationship between:
25PA1140 and 25CV7033; and
26PA1024 and 26CV3064.


XV. PRIVACY OF FINANCIAL INFORMATION IN POVERTY AFFIDAVIT CASES
The requester recognizes that Poverty Affidavits/applications may contain personal financial information that may be protected from disclosure by applicable law.
This request therefore does not seek disclosure of protected personal financial information merely because it appears within a Poverty Affidavit or related financial document.
Instead, the requester seeks the administrative records concerning the receipt, processing, routing, indexing, docketing, status, disposition, and handling of those Poverty Affidavit proceedings.
If a responsive record contains protected financial information or other exempt information, please redact only the portion for which a specific legal exemption applies and provide the remaining segregable information.
A determination that a financial document itself, or a portion of it, is exempt should not be treated as a basis for withholding separate administrative records concerning the processing of that document.
If the Clerk determines that any portion of a Poverty Affidavit record cannot be disclosed, please identify the specific statutory authority, subsection, and paragraph supporting the withholding and provide all reasonably segregable nonexempt portions.


XVI. COMMUNICATIONS CONCERNING POVERTY AFFIDAVIT PROCESSING
For 25PA1140 and 26PA1024, please provide existing administrative communications concerning:
receipt;
filing;
docketing;
processing;
routing;
status;
deficiencies;
corrections;
fee issues;
judicial routing;
administrative disposition;
and communications concerning the relationship between each Poverty Affidavit proceeding and its associated principal case.

Again, the request does not seek protected financial information itself except to the extent disclosure is otherwise authorized by law. The request is principally for the administrative record concerning how the Poverty Affidavit proceeding was processed.


XVII. COURT AND JUDICIAL-OFFICE ADMINISTRATIVE COMMUNICATIONS
Please provide existing records reflecting communications involving the Clerk's Office, Clerk personnel, court administrators, case-management personnel, judicial assistants, or other court personnel concerning the administrative handling of the four identified cases.

This includes communications concerning:
filing receipt;
docketing;
routing;
scheduling;
service;
notification;
administrative status;
filing deficiencies;
IFP/Poverty Affidavit processing;
assignment or reassignment;
case-management functions;
and other ministerial matters.

The requester expressly excludes judicial deliberations, judicial mental impressions, draft rulings, legal analysis, and protected judicial work product.
The fact that a communication involves a judge, judicial assistant, court administrator, or other judicial-office employee does not by itself define the nature of the record requested. The request is directed to existing records concerning administrative or ministerial functions, to the extent disclosure is permitted by law.


XVIII. POLICIES, PROCEDURES, TRAINING, AND SYSTEM DOCUMENTATION
Please provide current and, where maintained and applicable during the relevant period, policies, procedures, manuals, instructions, forms, training materials, administrative guidance, workflow documentation, or system documentation governing:
filing;
acceptance and rejection;
docketing;
document indexing;
electronic filing;
electronic service;
recipient-list management;
routing;
case assignment;
reassignment;
administrative holds;
filing corrections;
IFP/Poverty Affidavit processing;
communication with self-represented litigants;
communication with attorneys;
administrative notes;
case-management entries;
electronic audit histories;
service transactions;
and retention of administrative records.

Where a policy, procedure, workflow, or system practice changed during the relevant period, please provide the applicable version or versions maintained by the Clerk's Office.


XIX. RECORD RETENTION, PRESERVATION, AND ELECTRONIC HISTORY
Please provide existing retention schedules, policies, procedures, administrative guidance, or other records governing retention or preservation of records responsive to this request, including:
filing receipt records;
docketing records;
administrative notes;
electronic case-management histories;
service/notification histories;
recipient-list histories;
email communications;
telephone/contact records;
routing records;
audit logs;
workflow histories;
document histories;
and other administrative records concerning the four identified cases.

Please also provide existing records concerning deletion, overwriting, archival, transfer, or disposition of such administrative records, where responsive and maintained.
This request does not ask the Clerk's Office to create a new retention report.


XX. ELECTRONIC RECORD FORMAT AND EXISTING DATA FIELDS
Where responsive information is maintained electronically, please provide it electronically.
Where reasonably available, please provide electronic records in the format in which they are maintained or in a standard export format supported by the existing system.

The requester specifically requests existing responsive:
data fields;
timestamps;
transaction histories;
audit histories;
workflow histories;
status fields;
user/action fields;
recipient fields;
service transaction fields;
document-history fields;
and other existing electronic metadata or system-generated information.

The requester recognizes that Georgia law does not require creation of a new report, summary, or compilation that did not exist when the request was made. The request is therefore intentionally framed to seek existing records and data maintained in the ordinary course.

Where existing software ordinarily used by the Clerk's Office can retrieve responsive data through existing search, filter, range, query, report, or export functions, please use those existing capabilities to the extent required by applicable law.


XXI. RECORDS MAINTAINED BY THIRD-PARTY VENDORS OR THE GEORGIA SUPERIOR COURT CLERKS' COOPERATIVE AUTHORITY
If responsive records are maintained electronically by a third-party service provider, electronic filing vendor, document-management provider, case-management provider, archival provider, or the Georgia Superior Court Clerks' Cooperative Authority on behalf of the Clerk, please treat those records as records maintained for or on behalf of the Clerk to the extent provided by applicable law.

If the Clerk's Office does not physically possess a responsive record but is the custodian or responsible public-records authority for records maintained on its behalf, please process the request accordingly.

The requester is not asking the Clerk's Office to obtain records from another independent governmental agency that is itself the proper custodian where applicable. This provision is intended to prevent records maintained electronically for the Clerk from being excluded merely because they are stored or archived through another system or provider.


XXII. RECORDS ALREADY AVAILABLE ON THE PUBLIC DOCKET
The requester recognizes that some basic filing information is available through the public docket.

This request nevertheless seeks additional existing administrative records that may not be displayed on the public docket, including:
receipt timestamps;
processing timestamps;
electronic transaction information;
acceptance/rejection history;
workflow history;
routing history;
administrative notes;
audit information;
user/action information;
service-recipient history;
service transaction information;
historical changes to recipient lists;
internal administrative communications;
telephone/contact records;
case-management status information;
and other existing administrative information.

The fact that a corresponding filing, docket entry, or date appears publicly should therefore not be treated as a basis for declining to search for or produce separate responsive administrative records maintained in the Clerk's systems.


XXIII. EXISTING-RECORD LIMITATION
This request seeks existing records.

It does not ask the Clerk's Office to:
create a new report;
prepare a new chronology;
answer interrogatories;
provide legal advice;
provide a legal conclusion;
determine whether conduct was lawful or unlawful;
determine whether a person was entitled to service;
explain judicial decisions;
reconstruct undocumented events;
or create information that did not exist at the time of the request.

Where a requested fact is contained in an existing record, database field, audit record, administrative note, communication, system history, or other existing record, that existing record is requested. If a particular requested record does not exist, please state that the requested record does not exist rather than treating the request as asking the Clerk's Office to create a new record.


XXIV. SEGREGABILITY AND EXEMPTIONS
If a responsive record contains information that is exempt from disclosure, please redact only the portion for which a specific exemption applies and provide the remaining segregable portion.

For any record or portion withheld, please identify:
the record or category of record withheld;
the specific statutory authority for withholding;
the Code section;
subsection and paragraph, where applicable;
and whether reasonably segregable nonexempt information has been released.

The requester recognizes that particular categories of information may be protected by law, including certain personal financial information, privileged material, protected judicial deliberative material, or other specifically exempt information. The requester does not intend to seek disclosure of information that Georgia law expressly protects.
At the same time, an exemption applicable to one portion of a record should not be treated as an exemption applicable to the entire record where nonexempt information can reasonably be segregated.


XXV. REQUEST FOR RESPONSE AND PRODUCTION
Please provide responsive records electronically through the MuckRock request portal or by another electronic method reasonably available to the Clerk's Office.
If responsive records cannot be produced within the statutory period, please provide the required description of the responsive records and the expected production timeline.
If the Clerk's Office determines that the request requires substantial search, retrieval, or redaction time, please provide the applicable cost estimate before incurring charges exceeding the statutory threshold.
If a search or production category is determined to be unduly broad, please identify the specific category at issue and explain what narrower identification would permit the records to be located. Please do not simply deny the entire request when responsive records can reasonably be identified and produced in categories or installments.
The requester is willing to receive responsive records on a rolling basis.


XXVI. REQUEST FOR PRESERVATION OF RESPONSIVE ELECTRONIC RECORDS
Because this request specifically seeks historical electronic information, including audit histories, service-recipient histories, administrative notes, communications, transaction records, and other records that may be subject to routine retention or system-management practices, please preserve existing responsive records pending completion of this request to the extent permitted and required by applicable records-retention obligations.

This request does not ask the Clerk's Office to create a preservation record or report.
It requests preservation of existing responsive records while the request is being processed.


XXVII. NO WAIVER OF RIGHTS
This records request is made independently of, and does not waive, any rights, remedies, claims, objections, defenses, procedural positions, or potential causes of action available to the requester or any party in the identified proceedings.

The request is not intended to ask the Clerk's Office to determine whether any legal remedy is available or whether any conduct was legally proper.

The purpose is to obtain the underlying administrative and electronic records necessary to establish the objective factual history of the filing, processing, routing, service, notification, case-management, and records-management activities associated with the four identified proceedings.

Thank you for your assistance and for processing this request under the applicable Georgia public-records and court-record provisions.

Respectfully,

